;; Units made from OCaml sources, checked against the Rocq semantics of
;; Flambda 2 as the examples of the directory above are (see README.md).
;;
;; For each X.ml here (with X.mli if there is one), X.fl is the term that
;; the compiler gives to Simplify, as from_ocaml.sh makes it, and X.expected
;; is what the test tool prints of it. Both are kept, so that the term can
;; be read without building anything. The rules, which gen_rules.sh writes
;; from the files that are here, make X.fl again and compare, and run the
;; tool on X.fl and compare. `dune promote` updates either.
;;
;; To add an example, add X.ml (and X.mli), X.fl and X.expected, and
;; nothing else. As in the directory above, the rules are included by
;; run/dune and not by this file.

(rule
 (deps
  (glob_files *.ml)
  (glob_files *.mli)
  (glob_files *.fl)
  (glob_files *.expected))
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   dune.inc
   (run sh %{dep:gen_rules.sh} %{deps}))))
